var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-aphrodite"]=(()=>{var n=Object.defineProperty;var i=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(o,e)=>{for(var a in e)n(o,a,{get:e[a],enumerable:!0})},u=(o,e,a,r)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let t of h(e))!d.call(o,t)&&t!==a&&n(o,t,{get:()=>e[t],enumerable:!(r=i(e,t))||r.enumerable});return o};var p=o=>u(n({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),o);var m={};l(m,{journey:()=>s});var s={id:"deity-aphrodite",kind:"deity",paths:["hellenic","roman"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/aphrodite",title:"Aphrodite: The Golden One",summary:"A lunar month with Aphrodite: her Near Eastern roots and sea-born myth, the Heavenly and the Common Aphrodite, her armed and sea-faring sides, and how to honour her with flowers, incense and honest love.",welcome:`This is a month of study and devotion with Aphrodite, the Greek goddess of desire, beauty, union and the bonds that hold people together. She is far bigger than romance. Greeks honoured her as a goddess of the sea and safe sailing, of city harmony and of magistrates, and in some places as an armed warrior. This journey introduces all of her.

It is a self-dedication, not an initiation. No priesthood stands behind it, and it does not replace the rites of any group that initiates. You do not need to be in a relationship or want one: devotion to Aphrodite can be about beauty, friendship, pleasure, the body and self-worth as much as about lovers.

You will need flowers, a little incense or perfume, honey and a place to make beautiful.`,about:"Aphrodite is one of the oldest and most powerful of the Olympians, and one with clear roots outside Greece. Her great early sanctuaries were on Cyprus and the island of Kythera, and the Greeks themselves said her worship came from the east; she shares much with the Phoenician Astarte and the Mesopotamian Ishtar. Hesiod tells that she was born from the sea foam; Homer makes her the daughter of Zeus and Dione. Athenians honoured her as Ourania, heavenly, and as Pandemos, of all the people, a civic goddess of the whole community. She was invoked by sailors as Euploia, \u201Cof fair voyages,\u201D and at Sparta and Kythera she was shown armed. Sappho prayed to her in some of the most beautiful lines in Greek. Today Hellenic polytheists honour her alongside the other gods with flowers, incense, honey and doves\u2019 images, and many modern witches and Pagans see her as a goddess of love, beauty and self-love. Her Roman counterpart, Venus, grew into a mother of the Roman people.",notes:["Aphrodite is not only a goddess of romance. This journey covers her sea, civic and armed sides as well.","The story of \u201Csacred prostitution\u201D in her temples is contested. Many historians now think it was exaggerated or never happened. The journey explains why.","This journey does not teach love spells aimed at a particular person. Her own myths show how desire forced on someone does harm.","Flower and perfume safety: lilies are deadly to cats, and many essential oils are harmful to cats and dogs. Chocolate is poisonous to dogs. Roses are safe."],holyDays:["noumenia"],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"Making something beautiful",intro:"Aphrodite responds to beauty and to care. So the journey begins by making a small beautiful space and introducing yourself, with flowers, a scent and a sweet libation.",days:2,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"aphrodite-threshold-beauty",title:"Find one beautiful thing",instructions:"Before the rite, go out and choose one beautiful thing for her: a flower, a shell, a ribbon, a ripe apple. Choose it slowly, the way you would choose a gift for someone you admire.",times:1,prompt:"What did you choose, and what made it beautiful to you?"}],rite:{title:"An introduction with flowers",summary:"Purify, begin with Hestia, greet Aphrodite with incense and flowers, pour honeyed water or wine, and introduce yourself.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of fresh water and a match for khernips",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A candle or lamp"},{name:"Fresh flowers, ideally roses or myrtle",ref:"traditional/herb/rose"},{name:"Frankincense or a sweet incense",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense"},{name:"Water or wine with a spoon of honey"},{name:"A dish or bowl for the libation",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"The beautiful thing you chose"}],preparation:"Clean a small surface and lay a cloth on it. Set the flowers in water, the candle, the incense and the libation. Bathe or wash, and put on something you feel good in. Aphrodite was adorned by the Hours at her birth; it is fitting to come to her cared for.",steps:[{title:"Make khernips",instructions:"Quench the lit match in the water, wash your hands and touch a few drops to your face.",spoken:"With clean hands and a glad heart I come."},{title:"Begin with Hestia",instructions:"Light the candle and pour a drop of the libation for Hestia.",spoken:"Hestia, first and last: I begin with you.",light:!0},{title:"Call Aphrodite",instructions:"Light the incense. Raise your hands, palms up, and speak warmly.",spoken:"Aphrodite, golden one, Kypris, Kythereia, foam-born, laughter-loving: I am [your name]. I bring you flowers and sweet smoke and this small beautiful thing. I have come to learn who you are, all of you, and not only the part the songs remember. Smile on me as I learn.",minutes:2},{title:"Give and pour",instructions:"Place the beautiful thing beside the flowers. Pour most of the honeyed libation into the dish. Sit with the scent and the colour for a few minutes.",minutes:5,timed:!0},{title:"Close with Hestia",instructions:"Pour the last drop for Hestia, thank them both, and put out the flame.",spoken:"Hestia, last as you were first. Aphrodite, thank you.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Pour the libation outside onto earth. Keep the flowers on the shrine while they are fresh and compost them when they fade. Write a few lines about how it felt to come to her adorned.",safety:"Never leave a candle or incense unattended. Keep lilies, which are deadly to cats even in small amounts of pollen or water, out of the house if you have cats; roses and myrtle are safer. Use incense sparingly near pets and anyone with asthma."}},{kind:"study",title:"Learning her",intro:"Ten days of reading: her eastern roots and two births, her many titles and what Ourania and Pandemos really meant, the myths that show her power, and how she was worshipped and is honoured today.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"aphrodite-origins",title:"Born from the foam, come from the east",summary:"Aphrodite\u2019s two birth stories, her island homes, and her Near Eastern relatives.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The foam",body:`Hesiod\u2019s Theogony tells the most famous birth. When Kronos castrated his father Ouranos and threw the severed parts into the sea, \u201Ca white foam spread around them from the immortal flesh, and in it there grew a maiden.\u201D She drifted first to Kythera, then to Cyprus, and \u201Ccame forth an awful and lovely goddess, and grass grew up about her beneath her shapely feet.\u201D

Hesiod explains her names from the story: Aphrodite from aphros, foam; Kythereia from Kythera; Kyprogenes, Cyprus-born. Eros and Desire walked beside her from the start. Her portion among gods and mortals, he says, is \u201Cthe whisperings of maidens and smiles and deceits with sweet delight and love and graciousness.\u201D`},{heading:"The daughter of Zeus",body:`Homer tells it differently. In the Iliad, Aphrodite is the daughter of Zeus and the goddess Dione. When she is wounded on the battlefield at Troy, she runs crying to her mother. So Greeks had two Aphrodites from the beginning: a primeval power older than Zeus, born from the sky\u2019s own body, and a daughter in the Olympian family.

Later philosophers made a lot of this difference, as the next lesson shows. For now, notice that both stories were told side by side, and nobody felt they had to choose.`},{heading:"Cyprus and the east",body:`Her oldest great sanctuary was at Palaipaphos, Old Paphos, on Cyprus, where she was worshipped not as a statue but as a tall conical stone, an aniconic image. Roman coins still show it centuries later. The historian Herodotus wrote that the oldest temple of Aphrodite Ourania was at Ascalon in Syria-Palestine, and that the temples on Cyprus and Kythera were founded from it, Kythera by Phoenicians.

Scholars agree that Aphrodite has close relatives in the Near East: the Phoenician Astarte and the Mesopotamian Ishtar, goddesses of love, sex and war, linked with the evening star and doves. How exactly the Greek goddess grew from them is debated. Some see her as essentially an eastern goddess adopted by Greeks; others think a Greek or Indo-European goddess, perhaps related to the dawn, merged with eastern ones on Cyprus. Either way, she came to Greece by sea, through trade and shared worship, and that suits her.`},{heading:"Golden and laughing",body:"The epithets poets give her tell you how she was felt: golden (chryse\u0113), laughter-loving (philommeid\u0113s), violet-crowned, rich-crowned. The short Homeric hymn to her says \u201Csmiles are ever on her lovely face, and lovely is the brightness that plays over it.\u201D Brightness, smiling and gold come up again and again. When you picture her this month, picture her laughing."}],knowAndDont:{known:"Hesiod (Theogony 188\u2013206) and Homer (Iliad 5) give the two births. Herodotus (1.105) reports the eastern origin of her temples. The aniconic stone at Paphos is attested on coins and by ancient writers. Her parallels with Astarte and Ishtar are widely recognised.",unknown:"Exactly how Aphrodite came into Greek religion, and whether any part of her is originally Greek or Indo-European, is still debated. Herodotus was reporting what he was told, not what he had verified."},inPractice:"Many devotees keep a shell, a stone or a small image of the sea on her shrine to honour the foam birth, and some keep a smooth conical stone in memory of Paphos.",reflect:["Aphrodite is born from violence and becomes the goddess of love. What do you make of that?","What does golden, laughing love look like in your life?"],further:["Hesiod, Theogony 188\u2013206, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914)","Herodotus, Histories 1.105","Rachel Rosenzweig, Worshipping Aphrodite: Art and Cult in Classical Athens (2004)","Stephanie Budin, The Origin of Aphrodite (2003)"],library:["traditional/deity/aphrodite","traditional/deity/venus"]},{id:"aphrodite-titles",title:"Ourania, Pandemos, and the armed Aphrodite",summary:"What her famous pair of titles meant in cult and in philosophy, and her many other faces.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Two Aphrodites in Plato",body:`In Plato\u2019s Symposium, written in the fourth century BCE, a speaker named Pausanias argues that there are two Aphrodites. Ourania, the Heavenly, is the motherless daughter of Ouranos, and her love is noble and lasting. Pandemos, the Common, is the daughter of Zeus and Dione, and her love is bodily and casual. This speech has shaped how people have read the two names ever since.

But it is one character\u2019s argument at a drinking party, not a description of Greek religion. In actual cult, both titles meant something rather different.`},{heading:"Two Aphrodites in Athens",body:`In Athens, Aphrodite Pandemos was honoured near the Acropolis as the goddess \u201Cof all the people.\u201D Pausanias the travel writer says her worship was founded by the hero Theseus when he united the villages of Attica into one city. She was a civic goddess, the love and concord that bind citizens together, and Athenian magistrates made offerings to her. An Athenian decree of 283/2 BCE orders the city\u2019s officials to provide a dove to purify her shrine when her procession is held, to whitewash the altars and to wash the statues.

Aphrodite Ourania also had a sanctuary in Athens and was honoured widely across Greece, often as a stately, older power linked to the eastern goddess. So in religion, neither title was the \u201Clower\u201D love. Both were great.`},{heading:"The armed goddess and the sea",body:`At Sparta and on Kythera, Aphrodite was shown armed, as Areia or Hoplismene. In ports she was Euploia, goddess of fair sailing, and Pontia, of the open sea; sailors made offerings to her for safe voyages. At Thebes there were statues of Aphrodite Ourania, Pandemos and Apostrophia, the one who turns people away from unlawful desires, said to have been dedicated by Harmonia, Aphrodite\u2019s daughter.

She was also Epistrophia, the turner of hearts; Melainis, the dark one, a goddess of the night; and en K\u0113pois, \u201Cin the gardens,\u201D at a shrine outside Athens. Each title is a different power: desire, union, safety at sea, restraint, the dark. A well-made prayer to her uses the title that fits the request.`},{heading:"Names for prayer",body:"For your own prayers, good titles to learn are Kypris and Kythereia (the Cyprian, the Kytherean, her most common poetic names), Ourania, Pandemos, Euploia, Philommeides (laughter-loving), Chrys\u0113 (golden) and Apostrophia. You might call her Pandemos when you pray for harmony in a household, a friendship group or a community, and Euploia before a journey."}],knowAndDont:{known:"Plato\u2019s Symposium (180d\u2013181c) gives the philosophical distinction. Pausanias\u2019 Description of Greece reports the Athenian cults of Pandemos (1.22.3) and Ourania (1.14.7), the Theban statues (9.16.3) and the armed Aphrodite at Kythera (3.23.1). An Athenian decree records the dove purification of Pandemos\u2019 temple. Polemon (via the Suda, s.v. nephalia) reports wineless offerings to Ourania.",unknown:"The details of most of these local cults, including what was said and how often they were honoured, are lost. Later readers often confuse the Symposium\u2019s argument with how worshippers actually saw her."},inPractice:"Hellenic polytheists often address Aphrodite with several of these titles in one prayer. Many keep the civic Pandemos in mind by dedicating acts that build community, such as hosting a meal for friends or mending a quarrel.",reflect:["Which of her titles surprised you? Which do you want to pray to first?","Where is the love that holds your community together?"],further:["Plato, Symposium 180d\u2013181c","Pausanias, Description of Greece 1.14, 1.22, 3.23, 9.16","Vinciane Pirenne-Delforge, L\u2019Aphrodite grecque (1994), the standard study"],library:["traditional/deity/aphrodite"]},{id:"aphrodite-myths",title:"Her myths, and what they warn",summary:"Ares and Hephaistos, Anchises, Adonis, Paris and Helen, Hippolytus: the power of desire and its dangers.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The net and the laughter",body:`In the Odyssey, the bard Demodokos sings of Aphrodite\u2019s affair with Ares while she is married to the craftsman god Hephaistos. Hephaistos forges an invisible net, catches the two lovers in bed and calls the gods to see. The gods roar with laughter. Hermes jokes that he would gladly take Ares\u2019 place.

The story is comic and adult. It shows that Greeks did not expect Aphrodite to be faithful or demure. Her power is desire itself, and desire does not respect every rule. It also shows her bond with Ares: love and war, joined, with a daughter called Harmonia, harmony.`},{heading:"Anchises and Adonis",body:`The long Homeric Hymn to Aphrodite tells how Zeus made Aphrodite fall for a mortal, the herdsman Anchises, so that she too would know what it is to love someone who will die. She comes to him on Mount Ida disguised as a girl, they lie together, and then she reveals herself in terror and glory. Their son is Aeneas, ancestor of the Romans.

Adonis was a beautiful young man she loved, killed by a boar. Greek women mourned him each summer at the Adonia, planting quick-growing seeds in pots on rooftops, the \u201Cgardens of Adonis,\u201D and letting them wither, with songs of lament. The Adonia came from the east, like Aphrodite herself, and Sappho\u2019s songs already lament Adonis. Its date in Athens is debated; it is usually placed in summer. These are stories of a goddess who grieves.`},{heading:"Paris, Helen and Hippolytus",body:`When three goddesses competed for a golden apple, the Trojan prince Paris chose Aphrodite, who promised him the most beautiful woman in the world. That woman was Helen, already married to Menelaus of Sparta. The Trojan War followed. Greek poets argue for centuries about whether Helen chose or was compelled, and Aphrodite\u2019s gift looks very different depending on the answer.

In Euripides\u2019 Hippolytus, a young man devoted to the virgin huntress Artemis scorns Aphrodite entirely. Aphrodite makes his stepmother Phaedra fall in love with him, and everyone is destroyed. The play is not saying Hippolytus deserved it. It is saying that desire is a real power in human life, and that refusing to honour it at all is dangerous.`},{heading:"What the myths teach",body:`Taken together, these stories give a clear and adult picture. Aphrodite is joy, beauty and laughter. She is also a force that can overwhelm, deceive and destroy, especially when it is used on someone against their will. The myths do not pretend otherwise, and neither should we.

That is why this journey does not teach spells to make a particular person love you. Helen and Phaedra are warnings about desire forced on people. Ask Aphrodite instead to make you more loving, more beautiful to yourself, braver in your relationships and wiser in choosing them.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The Ares story is in Odyssey 8.266\u2013366; Anchises in Homeric Hymn V; the Adonia in Sappho\u2019s fragments, Aristophanes and Plutarch; Hippolytus in Euripides (428 BCE). The Judgement of Paris was told in lost epics and many later sources.",unknown:"We do not know how ordinary worshippers related these myths to their prayers. Poetry and cult often ran on separate tracks. The Roman story of Cupid and Psyche (Apuleius, second century CE) and Ovid\u2019s Pygmalion are later Roman tales, beautiful but not Greek cult."},inPractice:"Modern devotees often honour Adonis at midsummer with a small pot of fast-growing seeds such as lettuce or fennel, left to grow and fade, as a gentle rite of grief and beauty. Many read Sappho\u2019s poems aloud as offerings.",reflect:["Which of her myths unsettles you? What is it showing you about desire?","What would you ask her for, if not to make someone love you?"],further:["Homer, Odyssey book 8","Homeric Hymn V to Aphrodite, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914)","Euripides, Hippolytus","Sappho, Poems and Fragments, trans. Josephine Balmer or Anne Carson"],library:["traditional/deity/aphrodite","traditional/deity/artemis"]},{id:"aphrodite-worship",title:"How she was worshipped, and how she is honoured now",summary:"Incense, doves, flowers and the shape of her rites; the sacred prostitution debate; modern communities and her shrine.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Incense, flowers and doves",body:`In one of Sappho\u2019s fragments, the poet calls Aphrodite from Crete to a grove of apple trees, where altars smoke with frankincense, cold water murmurs through the branches and the ground is shaded with roses. That is a fair picture of her cult: incense, flowers, water, gardens.

Her birds were doves and sparrows, and images of doves, real doves in some sanctuaries, and dove offerings were common. Myrtle, roses and apples were hers. Pigs, the usual sacrifice for many gods, were usually not given to her. Wine was poured for her in many places, but the Hellenistic antiquarian Polemon, as reported in the Suda and in scholia on Sophocles, says that Athenians gave Aphrodite Ourania nephalia, wineless offerings, as they did for Mnemosyne, the Muses, Eos, Helios, Selene and the Nymphs. Honey mixed with water or milk is the classic wineless libation, which makes it a good choice for her Ourania side. Little terracotta figurines, mirrors and jewellery were common dedications.`},{heading:"The sacred prostitution question",body:`You will read that Aphrodite\u2019s temples, especially at Corinth, kept sacred prostitutes. This comes mainly from Strabo, writing around the turn of the era about a Corinth long before his time, and from Herodotus\u2019 story about Babylon. Many historians, most thoroughly Stephanie Budin, now argue that ritual sex work in Greek temples is poorly supported, perhaps a misunderstanding or a moralising story about foreigners.

Sex workers certainly honoured Aphrodite, as did brides, sailors and magistrates. But do not build your devotion on a story that may not be true. It is fair to say: we do not know, and the evidence is weaker than the legend.`},{heading:"Who honours her today",body:`Hellenic polytheists honour Aphrodite as one of the Olympians, with khernips, incense, flowers and libations, and keep the fourth of each lunar month as a day for her and her son Eros. Eclectic Pagans and witches often see her as a goddess of love, beauty and self-love, and honour her on Fridays, a custom that comes from the Roman day of Venus (dies Veneris), not from Greece.

Many queer devotees honour her as the goddess Sappho prayed to. Roman polytheists honour Venus at the Veneralia on 1 April and the Vinalia. All these approaches are real. The thing they share is that she is honoured with beauty, pleasure and generosity, not grim duty.`},{heading:"Her shrine",body:"Make her shrine beautiful: a cloth you love, fresh flowers, a shell, a mirror, a small dish for honey or sweets, an incense holder and a candle. Keep it fresh. Wilted flowers left too long are the opposite of what she is. A rose quartz or a pearl is a modern touch many people enjoy. Change something on it each week, even just the water in the vase."}],knowAndDont:{known:"Sappho\u2019s fragment 2 describes an altar with frankincense and roses. Doves, myrtle and apples are well attested as hers. Strabo (8.6.20) and Herodotus (1.199) are the main sources for the prostitution story, and Budin\u2019s The Myth of Sacred Prostitution (2008) argues against it.",unknown:"The prostitution question is not settled. Friday devotion is Roman and modern, not Greek. Crystal and colour correspondences are modern. We do not know the words most worshippers used."},inPractice:"Set up her shrine this week. Choose a regular day: the fourth of the lunar month if you follow the Hellenic calendar, or Friday if you prefer the Roman and modern custom. On that day, freshen the flowers, burn a little incense and pour honeyed water.",reflect:["How do you honour beauty in your daily life?","What do you feel about the sacred prostitution debate, and why?"],further:["Sappho, fragments 1 and 2","Stephanie Budin, The Myth of Sacred Prostitution in Antiquity (2008)","Rachel Rosenzweig, Worshipping Aphrodite (2004)"],library:["traditional/deity/aphrodite","traditional/deity/venus","traditional/herb/rose","traditional/herb/frankincense","traditional/crystal/rose_quartz"],premade:["cosmic-friday-venus-love","folk-khernips-libation"]}],practices:[{id:"aphrodite-study-sappho",title:"Read Sappho to her",instructions:"Once in this stage, light incense and read aloud a translation of Sappho\u2019s prayer to Aphrodite (fragment 1) and her grove poem (fragment 2). Reading poetry aloud was a real offering.",times:1,prompt:"What does Sappho ask Aphrodite for? What would you ask?"},{id:"aphrodite-study-shrine",title:"Build her shrine",instructions:"After the fourth lesson, set up a beautiful shrine with flowers, a shell or mirror, a dish and incense.",times:1,prompt:"What did you put on it? How does it feel to look at?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Nine nights of beauty",intro:"Nine nights of a short offering of beauty, and a lesson on her days, offerings and the ethics of love work.",days:12,lessons:[{id:"aphrodite-days-offerings",title:"Her days, her offerings, and love done well",summary:"The fourth of the month, the Aphrodisia, what to give, and the ethics of love magic and the body.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The fourth day and the Aphrodisia",body:`In the Athenian month, the fourth day was sacred to Aphrodite and Eros, as well as to Hermes and Herakles. The Noumenia, the first sighting of the new crescent, counts as day one, so if you mark the Noumenia in your calendar, the fourth day is three days later. A small offering of flowers, incense and honey on that day each month is a good rhythm.

Many cities held an Aphrodisia, a festival of Aphrodite, at different times. In Athens, the temple of Aphrodite Pandemos was purified before her procession, which some scholars place on the fourth of Hekatombaion, in midsummer, though the date is not certain. The Adonia also fell in summer. Romans kept the Veneralia on 1 April. None of these is a fixed holy day in the app\u2019s calendar, so choose one or two that speak to you and mark them yourself.`},{heading:"What to give",body:`Traditional offerings are frankincense and other sweet incense, roses, myrtle, apples, honey, honeyed cakes, wine, perfume, and small lovely things like a ribbon or a piece of jewellery. A dove image or feather can stand in for the doves once offered. Music, dance, poetry and the care of your own body offered in her name are all fitting.

Modern devotees often offer chocolate, which is lovely if no dog can reach it. Leave food on the shrine for a day, then compost it or put it outdoors where it is safe. Offerings to Aphrodite, an Olympian, may be shared in a meal in Hellenic custom, so some people eat the cake together after the rite as a celebration.`},{heading:"Love magic",body:`Ancient people did cast love spells, some in Aphrodite\u2019s name and many more in others\u2019, and some of those spells are coercive and ugly to read. Modern witchcraft is split on love magic. This app\u2019s line is clear: no spells to bend a particular person\u2019s will. Ask for love to come, for your heart to open, for a relationship to grow honest, for your own beauty to be seen, for a quarrel to mend. Those are prayers she can answer without anyone being harmed.

If you are hurting from a breakup, Aphrodite can be asked for healing as well. Sappho\u2019s first poem is exactly that: a prayer from someone whose love is not returned, asking the goddess to stand beside her.`},{heading:"The body and its worth",body:`Aphrodite is often sold as a goddess of looks, and the internet is full of \u201Cglamour\u201D work that can slide into shame about bodies that do not fit a picture. Her ancient worshippers were old and young, rich and poor, sailors and brides. The beauty she gives is radiance, the brightness that plays over a face when someone is loved and glad.

Treat care for your body as worship, not as a test. Wash, dress, rest, eat well, wear what you love. If her devotion ever makes you feel worse about yourself, stop and ask what you have mixed up with her.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The fourth day of the month was sacred to Aphrodite and Eros in Athenian tradition. An Athenian decree (IG II\xB2 659, 283/2 BCE) records the dove purification of Pandemos\u2019 temple before her procession. Love spells naming her and other gods survive in the Greek Magical Papyri and curse tablets.",unknown:"The exact date and form of many local Aphrodisia are uncertain, including the date of the Pandemos procession in Athens. Whether her offerings were usually shared or not likely depended on the rite."},inPractice:"Mark the next Noumenia and count four days for her monthly day. On that day, freshen the shrine, give incense and honeyed water, and do one act of real care for yourself or someone else. Use the app\u2019s self-love mirror rite if you want a guided form.",reflect:["What kind of love do you want to ask her for?","How do you treat your body? How would you treat it if it were a gift to her?"],further:["Christopher Faraone, Ancient Greek Love Magic (1999)"],library:["traditional/deity/aphrodite","traditional/crystal/rose_quartz"],premade:["kitchen-solitary-self-love-mirror","kitchen-honey-sweetening-cake","folk-noumenia-blessing"]}],practices:[{id:"aphrodite-nine-nights",title:"Nine nights of beauty",instructions:"For nine nights, wash your hands, light her candle and a little incense, and give her one small beautiful thing: a fresh flower, a drop of perfume on a cloth (not on the flame), a line of poetry, a song. Then name one thing you found beautiful or loving that day. Sit for three minutes, thank her and put out the flame.",times:9,every:"day",prompt:"What was beautiful today? Where did you see love?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"Looking in the mirror",intro:`Take a few days to look honestly at the month. Aphrodite is easy to be dazzled by, and that is exactly why this pause matters.

Stopping here is a good outcome. So is honouring her now and then without dedicating, or realising another god is calling more strongly. Hippolytus\u2019 mistake was refusing to honour Aphrodite at all; there is no mistake in honouring her without a vow.`,days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"aphrodite-discern-letter",title:"A letter to her",instructions:"Write Aphrodite a letter: what you came for, what you have found, what you are afraid of. Then read it back as if a friend had written it.",times:1,prompt:"Reading your letter back, what do you notice?"}],questions:["Am I drawn to Aphrodite herself, or mainly to the hope that she will bring me a particular person?","Have I met all of her this month, including her civic, sea and fierce sides, and do I still want to go on?","Has this devotion made me kinder to my body and to others? If not, what needs to change?","Can I keep a monthly day for her, and a fresh shrine, for at least a year?","Would I be relieved or sad if I waited? What does that tell me?"]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The golden promise",intro:`If you have chosen to go on, make your dedication on her fourth day of the month, or on a day that matters to you. This is a self-dedication: a promise you make yourself. It is not an initiation and does not make you a priestess. Groups that initiate have their own rites, and this does not replace them.

It is fitting to come adorned, after a bath, in clothes you love, with fresh flowers.`,days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-dedication to Aphrodite",summary:"Bathe and adorn yourself, purify, begin with Hestia, call Aphrodite by her titles, make your promise with roses and incense, and share a sweet in celebration.",minutes:45,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of fresh water and a match for khernips",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A candle"},{name:"Roses or myrtle, fresh",ref:"traditional/herb/rose"},{name:"Frankincense or another sweet incense",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense"},{name:"Wine or water with honey"},{name:"A honey cake or sweet",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"A small ornament to wear afterwards: a ribbon, ring or pendant"},{name:"Your written promise"}],preparation:"Clean and freshen her shrine. Take a bath or shower, and dress in something you feel lovely in. Write a promise in your own words: for example, to keep her monthly day, to honour beauty and love in your life, to treat your body with care, to love honestly and never to use her name to force anyone. Lay the ornament on the shrine.",steps:[{title:"Purify",instructions:"Make khernips, wash your hands and touch the water to your face and throat.",spoken:"Clean and adorned, I come to the golden one."},{title:"Begin with Hestia",instructions:"Light the candle and pour a drop for Hestia.",spoken:"Hestia, first and last: I begin with you.",light:!0},{title:"Call Aphrodite",instructions:"Light the incense. Raise your hands, palms up.",spoken:"Aphrodite, golden, laughter-loving, Kypris and Kythereia; Ourania of the heavens and Pandemos of all the people; Euploia of fair voyages: hear me. This month I have brought you flowers and smoke and songs and learned your stories. Now I bring you a promise.",minutes:2},{title:"Make your promise",instructions:"Read your promise aloud. Pour most of the libation for her. Lay a rose on the shrine. Put on the ornament: it is the sign of your promise.",spoken:"This I promise, freely and gladly. Let your brightness play over me as I keep it."},{title:"Celebrate",instructions:"Offer a piece of the cake on her dish. Then eat a piece yourself, slowly, enjoying it. Play a song you love, or sing, or simply sit and smile.",minutes:10},{title:"Close with Hestia",instructions:"Pour the last drop for Hestia, thank them both and put out the flame.",spoken:"Hestia, last as you were first. Aphrodite, thank you.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Pour the libation outside. Leave her piece of cake on the shrine until morning, then compost it. Wear the ornament when you want to remember her. Write down what you promised and how you felt.",safety:"Never leave the candle or incense unattended. Do not put perfume or oils on or near the flame. Keep lilies away from cats, and chocolate and grapes away from dogs. Use incense sparingly, with a window open."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Keeping her near",intro:`Your devotion now lives in the month and in daily life. Keep her fourth day with fresh flowers, incense and honey. Treat beauty, pleasure, friendship and love as things you can offer her. Keep the shrine fresh.

Once a year, on the day you dedicated or at the Aphrodisia you chose, renew your promise, change it or set it down with thanks. If you drift, bring her one flower and begin again. Aphrodite was never a goddess of grim duty.`,days:30,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"aphrodite-ongoing-fourth",title:"Her monthly day",instructions:"On the fourth day of each lunar month, freshen her shrine with flowers, incense and honeyed water.",times:12,every:"month",prompt:"Where was love and beauty this month?"},{id:"aphrodite-ongoing-rededicate",title:"Yearly rededication",instructions:"Repeat the dedication rite once a year with a renewed promise.",times:1,every:"month",prompt:"How has a year with Aphrodite changed how you love?"}]}],sources:["Hesiod, Theogony 188\u2013206, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), Project Gutenberg #348: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/348 (quoted: \u201Ca white foam spread around them from the immortal flesh, and in it there grew a maiden\u201D; \u201Ccame forth an awful and lovely goddess, and grass grew up about her beneath her shapely feet\u201D; \u201Cthe whisperings of maidens and smiles and deceits with sweet delight and love and graciousness\u201D)","Homeric Hymn X to Aphrodite, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), Project Gutenberg #348 (quoted: \u201Csmiles are ever on her lovely face, and lovely is the brightness that plays over it\u201D)","Homeric Hymns V and VI to Aphrodite, trans. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914), Project Gutenberg #348","Homer, Iliad 5 and Odyssey 8","Herodotus, Histories 1.105, 1.199; Strabo, Geography 8.6.20","Plato, Symposium 180d\u2013181c; Pausanias, Description of Greece 1.14.7, 1.22.3, 3.23.1, 9.16.3","Sappho, fragments 1 and 2","Athenian decree on the purification of the temple of Aphrodite Pandemos, IG II\xB2 659 (283/2 BCE)","Polemon, cited in the Suda, s.v. \u201Cnephalia,\u201D and the scholia on Sophocles, Oedipus at Colonus 100, on wineless offerings to Aphrodite Ourania","Vinciane Pirenne-Delforge, L\u2019Aphrodite grecque (1994)","Stephanie Budin, The Origin of Aphrodite (2003) and The Myth of Sacred Prostitution in Antiquity (2008)","Rachel Rosenzweig, Worshipping Aphrodite (2004)","Christopher Faraone, Ancient Greek Love Magic (1999)"]};return p(m);})();
